-- title:
--   LEMMA 1, p. 226 — R(t) = Y(t) − λtV(t) is a continuous-time martingale, in the form (5)
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $(\Omega,\mathcal F,P)$ is a probability space; $N=\{N(t),t\ge0\}$ is a process with values in a measurable space $S$; $A=\{A(t),t\ge0\}$ is a Poisson process at rate $\lambda>0$; $B\subseteq S$ is a set with $\{N(t)\in B\}\in\mathcal F$ for every $t\ge0$; $U(t)=\mathbf 1\{N(t)\in B\}$; the sample paths of $U$ are left continuous with right-hand limits w.p. 1; $U$ is jointly measurable on $\Omega\times[0,\infty)$; and lack of anticipation holds with respect to the joint history: for each $t\ge0$, $\{A(t+u)-A(t),u\ge0\}$ is independent of $\mathcal F_t$, the σ-field generated by $\{A(s),U(s);0\le s\le t\}$.
--
--   Let $R(t)=Y(t)-\lambda tV(t)$, $t\ge0$ (display (4)). Then $R$ is a continuous-time martingale with respect to its own history: every $R(t)$, $t\ge0$, is integrable, and for $t\ge0$, $h>0$, almost surely
--
--   $$E\{R(t+h)-R(t)\mid R(s),\,0\le s\le t\}=0.\qquad(5)$$
--
--   $R(t)$ compares the number of arrivals who find $N$ in $B$ with $\lambda$ times the time spent in $B$; Lemma 1 says the comparison has no drift, and Lemma 2 turns it into a strong law.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The conditioning σ-field is the one generated by $R(s)$, $0\le s\le t$, exactly as in (5); the stronger martingale property with respect to $\mathcal F_t$ is a consequence of (6) and is not stated here. Lack of anticipation is assumed against $\mathcal F_t$ rather than against $\{U(s),0\le s\le t\}$ alone as in the printed LAA (p. 225). The page's proof of (6) uses this joint independence ("from LAA and the independent-increments property … for any set in $\mathcal F_t$"), and the printed LAA together with independent increments does not give it: with $U=1$ on $[0,1]$, $U=G$ on $(1,2]$, $U=0$ afterwards, where $G$ is the parity of $A(2)-A(1)$ masked by a fair bit computed from the arrival times in $[0,1]$, $G$ is independent of the future increments after time $1$, so the printed LAA holds, yet $E\{R(2)-R(1)\mid R(s),s\le1\}=\pm\lambda e^{-2\lambda}\ne0$ on an event of positive probability. The joint condition implies the printed one.
-- source:
--   Wolff, Poisson Arrivals See Time Averages, Oper. Res. 30 (1982), DOI 10.1287/opre.30.2.223, p. 226, LEMMA 1 and display (5)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_WolffPASTA_Main_Setting

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter Topology

namespace WolffPASTA.Main

/-- LEMMA 1, p. 226, in the form (5): `R` is a continuous-time martingale, i.e. each `R(t)` is
integrable and `E{R(t + h) - R(t) | R(s), 0 ≤ s ≤ t} = 0` for `t ≥ 0`, `h > 0`.
Lack of anticipation is taken against the joint history `ℱₜ` (`LAAJoint`), the independence the
page's proof of (6) uses; the printed LAA (future increments independent of the past of `U` only)
does not imply (6) or LEMMA 1 (see the Formalization Note). -/
theorem lemma_1
    {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
    {S : Type*} [MeasurableSpace S] (N : ℝ → Ω → S)
    (A : ℝ → Ω → ℕ) (lam : ℝ) (hlam : 0 < lam) (hA : IsPoissonProcess P A lam)
    (B : Set S) (hB : ∀ t, 0 ≤ t → MeasurableSet {ω | N t ω ∈ B})
    (hpath : PathRegular P (U N B))
    (hjoint : JointlyMeasurable (U N B))
    (hLAA : LAAJoint P A (U N B)) :
    (∀ t, 0 ≤ t → Integrable (R (U N B) A lam t) P) ∧
      ∀ t h, 0 ≤ t → 0 < h →
        P[fun ω => R (U N B) A lam (t + h) ω - R (U N B) A lam t ω | Rhist (R (U N B) A lam) t]
          =ᵐ[P] 0 := by sorry

end WolffPASTA.Main
